Done. I created the spec at `.kiro/specs/civic-case-actions/` with the three files and wrote only those — no tasks were implemented.

What I did first: read the Spec 1 section and the "Property-based tests" section of `docs/kiro-brief.md`, the four steering docs, and the three engine files (`gate.py`, `approvals.py`, `targets/payments.py`) so the design is grounded in how the engine actually works.

**requirements.md** — 10 requirements in EARS format with numbered acceptance criteria:
- The four gated actions, simulated systems of record, the four pre-action rules (entitlement ceiling, one-reissue-per-period, eligibility/active at send time, live authority lease), routing to the `caseworkers` group with an SLA, MCP exposure, and verifiable receipts.
- Requirement 10 pulls in only the brief's PBT properties that apply to this spec: property 1 (at most one effect), property 5 (rules hold under concurrency), and property 2 (receipts never overclaim). I noted that the lifecycle-evidence (property 4) and ledger-reconciliation (property 3) properties belong to Spec 2, since this spec is the "was it the right action?" half.

**design.md** — reuses the engine explicitly: `Gate` for dispatch/premise re-validation/recovery/receipts, `approvals.Rule`/`Route`/`Authority`/`Inbox` for the pre-action decision and person queue, and one target per system modeled on `targets/payments.py` (payment rail = tier 1, scheduler and case records = tier 2). It maps the proposal/premise/lease concepts to civic terms and shows how "closed while deciding" and "second reissue after a crash" fall out of the gate's commit-time and recovery checks rather than a new state machine.

**tasks.md** — a numbered plan where every task (and sub-task) carries a `_Requirements:_` reference back to the criteria it satisfies.

A couple of things worth your review before running tasks: I inferred the module layout (`backend/civic/`, `interlock/targets/civic/`, a `CivicOffice` facade) from the brief and structure steering, and I set the entitlement/large-amount limit as a configurable value rather than a fixed number since the brief says "a set limit" without naming it.
